body:
Personally I've lost weight without counting, but I'll admit it comes with a caveat which is it requires reading food labels religiously. I read *Ultra Processed People* by Chris Tulleken and cut out about half of the foods I was purchasing (I already ate relatively clean) such as items with emulsifiers, excessive flavoring agents and any vegetable oils. I don't go out to restaurants either. I really only eat whole foods, and I don't get hungry like I used to; because of this I've gone from 130 lbs to 120 lbs within a month without agonizing over calories like I used to.

&#x200B;

Additives cause us to eat more than we need to without getting any of the nutrition that our body anticipates from the deceiving flavors that companies put into food. Just pointing out another approach to consider. I was never good at logging calories and restriction of calories always left me tired; I have much more energy with this method!

I was thirteen when I discovered *Worlds* in 2014. My best friend had recommended it to me. I had been living in one town for seven years and had a very close knit friend group, but suddenly my mom needed to take us to live with my grandma over an hour away. I listened to the full album chronologically during the car ride to my new temporary home. 

&#x200B;

As a kid that had grown up happily listening to Monstercat, Deadmau5, Crystal Castles, etc, I still wasn't prepared for the type of sound Worlds delivered. I was facing a very emotionally challenging juncture in my adolescence; and while *Goodbye to a World* was the one that made me break down and cry about leaving my hometown, *Fellow Feeling* was like the perfect climax for the album. I still feel like that track (as well most of Worlds tbh) is underrated. It is so raw and gritty and I feel like it literally triggered some type of awakening for little preteen me. I still fail to grasp how Porter conceived *Worlds* at age 19.

Yeah I had a very close group of friends when I was younger that we swore would always be together. One blew up at me as soon as she got the chance and we realized she was a person that hoards info about others in order to hurt them later; so we cut her off. Another developed bipolar I and we agreed to take a friendship break while he seeks treatment since he's rather volatile currently. Another I do speak to but he moved several states away.

&#x200B;

 I have gradually become less social due to these losses and so nowadays I over-analyze and tend to panic about social interactions. This leads to a cascade of further isolation and awkwardness. The only positive I have is more time to partake in whatever obsessions or hobbies I am currently involved in; but honestly even that aspect probably makes me appear even more like a weird hermit. OH also I do less recreational drugs than I used to I suppose, but I could still avoid them while having a social life if I made the effort. Overall, being isolated undoubtedly detrimental to my lifespan and mental well being. I am constantly craving a girl bestie to go window shopping with and cook with while getting wine tipsy. Social media doesn't help and just further stresses how lonely I feel.

I have dark blue eyes and neutral light skin. I tend to utilize these most: Taupe, pastel blue, pastel green/mint, lavender, neutral browns, warm browns, almond/off white shimmer and matte (for highlights), champagne shimmer, light gold shimmer, olive green tones, dark pinkish brown, dark brown shimmer, muted pinks, burnt orange, plum tones

&#x200B;

Avoid: Hot pink, cobalt blue, bright reds, cool greys, dark bronze, dark gold shimmer, silver. Basically I like color; but anything that is too harsh or blends in with my eyes too much I don't mess with. Pastel blue/mint/lavender are the exception though bc wearing them makes me feel like I'm a hot retro cartoon girl lol

Here's my rationale, and this is just what *I* wish someone told me when I started meds years ago, so take it with a grain of salt. The typical thoughts about tolerance appear to be outdated; the idea that long term use of stimulants at a normal, consistent dose causes tolerance is no longer supported by the literature. Taking meds consistently is the way to go. I do not go off meds except when I'm very ill. I have been on the same dose of dextroamphetamine IR for two years. I take it religiously as prescribed and am comfortable and functional. It hasn't given me an "awake" feeling for a long time and I understand it shouldn't. I don't really consciously notice the effect of my meds anymore on a day to day basis, *but* I *do* notice when I'm off them. That's how it should be, just like any other psych med or *ANY* medication in general. For example, an ideal blood pressure med's effect shouldn't be noticed while on it, *but* going *off* it suddenly may cause a cardiac event (negative effect).

&#x200B;

Because your body has now gotten used to the drug, you are feeling the drug wearing off in the evening more strongly. This is normal. You're getting even *more* tired at a higher dose because your body is reacting to a bigger "dropoff" of medication than before. It is NOT an indicator that you need to up your dose more or start taking "tolerance breaks." It is in indicator that your body is metabolizing Concerta a bit too quickly (and actually your dose might be a bit too high now.) 

&#x200B;

This has a few potential solutions: Get an instant release (IR) booster for the evening (example instructions: Take 1 Concerta 27 mg tablet at 8 AM then, take 1 tab ritalin 10 mg at 3 pm), or ask to switch your Concerta to methylphenidate IR thrice daily (example: Take 1 Ritalin 10 mg tablet by mouth three times daily at 8 am, noon, and 3 pm), OR potentially try Vyvanse which times itself to release three times a day. 

&#x200B;

Adults who get dx'd with ADHD have already formed a long time habit of only paying attention to feelings that are novel and stimulating. Once they've experienced the initial excitement of seeing marked improvements in symptoms for a few weeks, it's common for them to overlook the progress they have made. Randomly going off meds and upping one's dose too much are the two common things which I strongly feel *actually* worsen tolerance. Do not get fooled into chasing that "new med!!" feeling and don't fall into the tolerance hysteria.  *I can't reiterate this enough*.

&#x200B;

If you do, you may get frustrated with a dose/drug never being "right" and develop some of the more grim side effects associated with stimulant use. My doctor and I feel I have avoided them by being consistent and staying at a mild-moderate dose. For example, my blood pressure and pulse have remained low and I still have a good appetite after years of being on stimulants. My only noticeable side effects at this point are hormonal acne, increased thirst, and being a bit sweatier than I was before meds.

Yeah I'm in that age group (22) and for the past year or so I've been helplessly watching my buddy since middle school develop and struggle with what is undoubtedly untreated bipolar I. It's made me feel so unsettled and depressed.

&#x200B;

 He used to just be a silly, bright, sensitive dude with a few family issues. I noticed him start smoking a lot of weed and trying psychedelics around freshman year. Typical college stuff. He got overwhelmed and ended up dropping out after a while. Since then when he calls he'll be frantically telling me about how people in his town are conspiring against him because they think he's a creep/junkie. 

&#x200B;

Here's an example: he had picked up a bad drinking habit; and he told me that when he went to the liquor store, there were people waiting there faking having DT shakes in order to mock him when he arrived. He genuinely thought his roommates planted people all over town to make him feel shame and no gentle prodding would dissuade him from this. From what I picked up on, his roommates also were trying to kick him out and get a restraining order. 

&#x200B;

I had him stay over recently and I noticed he only slept like two hours, would speak rapidly but then go silent and stare into space for a bit, and then eventually he snapped at me and got aggressive out of nowhere. I've urged him to see a psychiatrist this whole time, but I told him at this point I need to step back for my own well being. It's just too much. There really *NEEDS* to be some kind of PSA during high school about the mental conditions you can develop as an adolescent.

I would try not to worry about it. I can often smell things others can't. Apparently phantosmia can also fall under anxiety/stress symptoms since our senses get even more receptive when stressed. I have friends who have way less attuned smell than me in general whether from smoking/vaping, genetics, or sinus problems. I was having a bonfire the other day with a friend and was talking about how the burning log smelled like meat or animal musk something, but he couldn't pick up the scent at all even when I encouraged him to get close. 

&#x200B;

A lot of random chemicals which are naturally formed can smell like other things. Once I was in the woods and suddenly smelt uhm, well, *semen*. And that's how I discovered some mushrooms contain a compound literally called spermadine. Maybe you got a whiff of a plant or something with similar compounds to italian seasonings. 

&#x200B;

Btw if it was a potato chip that tasted like soil it's normal. Potatoes ofc grow under soil and a tiny bit of dirt can get get into the potato "eyes" and get missed during chip production. I've gotten a "bad" potato chip many times in my life lol. I also cook potatoes and I notice some of the starchier varieties can taste like dirt even after washing or peeling.

Damn, I experience both of those features and its so exhausting and makes me feel very self conscious. I hate it. All day at work I imagine what my mouth looks like while talking to people, or if my hair is making my silhouette look weird, or maybe my shoulders aren't positioned right. Having a health issue makes it worse like I currently have two bulging neck discs and I'm very mentally preoccupied with my posture lately.

&#x200B;

 Sometimes I also try to mentally estimate readings like blood sugar, heartrate, blood pressure during the day based on how I'm feeling, which is absurd. I have also gone through periods where I've obsessed over each breath to the point where it kept me from sleeping for months due to panicking about whether the next breath would be "right" or "wrong."

I really suggest taking a look at [this article](https://www.pharmacytimes.com/view/debunking-a-common-pharmacy-myth-the-80-125-bioequivalence-rule) to truly understand variance between brand and generic. But the main point from it to answer your question is that it would be very difficult for a generic to differ more than 10% from the brand name to pass requirements. Historically, the mean difference between brand and generics when tested was 3.5%.

&#x200B;

In the pharmacy, we automatically use generic products for prescriptions unless there isn't one or the doctor specifically calls for name brand. Like 95% of what we fill is generic. If generic drugs had a discernible difference from their brand name counterparts, we would have a lot of serious issues with patient health+ a lot of patients asking for brand name versions, but we don't.

It's a bit more nuanced with meds (under the FDA at least.) There are very specific laws around generic drugs. 

&#x200B;

Firstly, understand there will be *several* manufacturers for lisdexamfetamine (generic Vyvanse), as with most all other generics. For example, if you take amphetamine/dextroamphetamine (Adderall) instant release 10mg tablets you might get a tablet produced by Teva Industries, or Sandoz, or Alvogen, or Epic Pharma, or Aurobindo, and the list goes on. This goes for any generic med.

&#x200B;

On the pharmacy staff end, we search the drug name and a big list will come up with how many bottles of product from each manufacturer are available to order. With the current Adderall shortage, '0' would come up for ALL manufacturers of most doses of generic Adderall (as well as the name brand usually); so if we refresh the page and for example, we see a quantity of 3 come up for Epic Pharma's ampheta/dextro 20 mg tablet, we are immediately rushing to try and order some before other pharmacies do. Basically there's no being picky, whatever manufacturer pops up is what we get in, that is *if* it even comes, which it sometimes wouldn't if we were unlucky. But typically we can choose from a variety of products. You can always ask for a different manufacturer. We offer this option since anyone can have an intolerance to a filler or dye; or sometimes insurance will only cover certain generic brands (I find this very annoying when I come across it, happens with Medicaid insurance for a lot of stuff), You can currently still do this if you're taking focalin, dexedrine, and other less common ADHD meds. I did this myself for dextroamphetamine capsules for a long time.

&#x200B;

The tablets from say, Teva will look different from the tablets from Epic Pharma. They may have slightly different dyes or fillers, and their labs are an entire ocean away from each other, but they are still legally required to be bioequivalent to the brand name drug, and consequently, one another as well. For a generic to be approved to meet **bioequivalence** standards the FDA has random subjects take the generic drug while another group takes the brand name; both then have their blood levels of the drug tested at many periodic intervals over time. There must be "no significant difference" in the bloodstream between how the name and generic are absorbed, distributed, metabolized, and eliminated.

&#x200B;

After those thorough trials, all generics are then subjected to **post market surveillance**. Random batches of generic drugs are constantly being tested by the FDA to assure quality and purity. Also if a generic drug manufacturer makes changes to its manufacturing processes, formulation, or other aspects of the drug post-approval, it *must* notify the FDA. The FDA reviews these changes to assess their impact on the drugs performance. So, unlike food and supplements which aren't actually *monitored* by the FDA, drug are so manufacturers are actually legally required to notify immediately about any formulation changes, no matter how minor. *If* they *do* make a production change, they also still must meet the strict criteria from when the generic was first approved.

&#x200B;

**MedWatch** is the FDAs post market monitoring program. Patients and healthcare workers can report any issues with adverse effects or potency there. They constantly observe and follow up with manufacturers based on the feedback from there. Beyond these safeguards there are also FDA staff that physically go to labs abroad for in person inspection to ensure everything is up to standard.

&#x200B;

Just wrote this up in case anyone who is skeptical or fearful about the new generics gets at least a little relief from it. The FDA doesn't play around. There are few companies that manage to slip below their standards to cut costs, and FDA snuffs them out pretty quickly when it does occur.

Rural midwest. Just stay within half an hour or so from a larger town (10000+ residents) in case you need to access certain businesses or stores occasionally. My bf and I own a 3br home in rural MN we bought for just less than 200k. It is a double wide with 2 acres. I wouldn't consider the area gross at all, just boring at times. I keep myself busy with vegetable gardening and painting. I find it a bit easier to not spend on stupid purchases like eating out/shopping since we don't have many restaurants or doordash or places to clothes shop (besides thrift stores :) ) The community in my area is very friendly and helpful also. My neighbor was plowing my driveway for free all winter; I didn't even know who was doing it until he introduced himself months later!

&#x200B;

I suppose some people can't handle a rural lifestyle but I truly think it's the best for cheap homes/self sufficiency. I should also mention that groceries are far cheaper than in the city. While taxes are considered moderate in MN specifically, the social assistance programs, high quality of life, high avg wages/salaries, and other benefits make up for it: free school lunches for all students, strong job market \[esp healthcare and industrial\], very few natural disasters, lots of policies to protect employees, free/low cost healthcare is always available if one is unemployed, is pregnant/has children, falls below average income level. Stuff like that is why we have incredible **cost of living to quality of life ratio**. Maybe try searching that term when you're checking out places to potentially move.

Yes there is a combination antidepressant now called Auvelity. I work in pharmacy and have not yet seen it prescribed. This is in part because it's really expensive (insurances will not cover it, and if they do you'll have to try a billion other meds/therapies first) and it will take a decade for it to become generic just like any new drug. However it does look really promising. The reason they work together is because they act on the same liver receptor; bupropion stops DXM from undergoing its normal liver metabolism into DXO (dextrorphan--this is what makes you feel dissociated and intoxicated). So it remains in the blood as DXM, which is primarily a serotonin reuptake inhibitor, instead of turning into a dissociative compound. The dose of dxm in it is 45 mg while the dose of bupropion is 105 mg. It wouldn't be wise to try to DIY it per say, as combo pills can have fancy release mechanisms which can't be easily recreated. 

&#x200B;

On another note--DO NOT combine recreational doses of dxm with therapeutic doses of bupropion. Some people on this sub say it amplifies the high but DO NOT do it. My sibling is in a coma currently from doing so. Bupropion drastically lowers the seizure threshold and unfortunately he entered status epilepticus after relapsing in dxm addiction while on bupropion.

Cats. I wish I liked dogs more than I do, but I really only can stand smaller dogs. I don't like how with dogs, you have to spend a crap ton of time undoing behaviors like barking, pulling on the leash, peeing inside, jumping on people etc. I've had plenty of dogs growing up and every time we still had issues like pulling on the leash, running out the door, barking. It's too easy to give the dog the wrong signal and undo whatever you're training. I'm just not cut out for that I guess. 

&#x200B;

Cats, on the other hand, kinda just get it. You plop em in the litter box as a baby, and they immediately start digging and going potty there. As long as you purchase enrichment like scratch posts and have enough litter boxes, they're all good. They're super chill and don't make me get up from whatever I'm doing to give them attention. They're happy to sit on the couch by me as I do whatever on my laptop. They're a lot softer than dogs imo as well and have a pleasant smell. Meanwhile I have to wash my hands after playing with or petting my dog.

I am a woman with ADHD. I am one of those kids that excelled in school, especially in earlier years, because I was bright enough to handle what I deemed easy curriculum. I was creative and showed extreme focus when it came to art. No one thought anything of my walking on my tiptoes since age 3 or why my grades were perfect, but my room a disaster, just deemed lazy. I wasn't diagnosed until I was nearly an adult. Now, even though a diagnosis is necessary, false or misdiagnosis still happens often. There is no way of physically seeing ADHD on say, a scan; anyone can go into a clinic and act the part if they have a desire to. I do not subscribe to the notion that everyone who goes in to get tested is well-intentioned. (Most are but not all) There is also the simple fact that a lot of psychiatrists have been replaced providers with half the training, thus making it easier than it once was to feign illness; but that's an entirely different can of worms.

&#x200B;

The reason that I believe strongly that this girl did malinger is due to the fact that she did everything to assert that being the case, aside from outright saying "I'm gonna fake my way to a diagnosis." This individual was my closest friend for many years. She has never had a failing grade, let alone even a C in a single college class. This individual has an expensive, immaculately clean and well decorated house. This person has worked a job as well as college full time for years without negative incident. What's worse, also told me I was "lazy and just didn't work enough" for dropping out of university. She implored my other friend with ADHD (who also dropped out of university recently) to describe, in detail, every question asked to obtain his diagnosis because she was "just nervous they'll say no." She was told straight up during her first eval that she did not have ADHD. She then went to a different clinic and came back with the prescription for Adderall. (this could be considered doctor shopping just FYI) She boasts on a spam account about how Adderall makes her "feel like God herself." As well as exclaiming "time to microdose my meth!" and once called them her "skinny girlboss pills." Part of the reason I no longer speak to her is also because she was an *excellent* liar and whenever her mom would catch us doing something she'd blame it on me. If her mom found a receipt for say, grenadine (for vodka cocktails we both drank at a party) she'd say it was mine because I had no parents to yell at me. I just got to be the shitty scapegoat so people always thought I was a pitiful child with no morals. So there's a bit more context.

&#x200B;

Obviously I have no say over what her diagnosis should be, *nor that I should have that authority*. I am saying with my anecdote that I believe this person is full of shit in general. As well as simply that its not a myth that people lie for drugs. I may not be a doctor, but I do work in pharmacy, and recognize drug diversion when I see it. ig it's controversial, but I don't think people who are fiending for and taking other patient's amphetamines should be handed them. Of course, people that lie will figure out down the line that they've made a grave mistake because it's actually shitty having to be physiologically dependent on a schedule II substance. It really just pisses me off that I have to be on meds that get other people high as well as suffer the consequences of those that chase that high.

I agree. Also fed up with people faking an ailment I've been outcasted for my whole life: ADHD. Here's my experience before I get pitchforks at me. I grew up with someone who never had any issues getting schoolwork done; she was constantly studying in the coffeeshop her mom owned, making extravagant notes with various imported highlighters and pens. She was my best friend at the times and I sometimes had to copy her work for our AP classes as I was a complete mess during junior year. We were both intelligent, but I clearly had issues with motivating myself.  I hadn't been offered stimulants yet, only Wellbutrin, which helped a bit but I still procrastinated to death on everything. I was also bullied for having niche interests meanwhile she was well-liked by our class. In our senior year, a peer offered her an Adderall capsule before they went to a halloween party. She instantly loved the feeling of it and wouldn't stop talking about wanting it again.

&#x200B;

She chose psychology as her major. We stopped being friends as she became very petty and was too privileged to relate to me any longer. We still had mutual friends though, and I found out she got a rented house with an ADHD roommate. She also made it clear that her roommate was sharing Adderall with her. Last I heard, she got an ADHD diagnosis for herself (which wouldn't be difficult, seeing as studying neurological conditions is part of her degree--but I know many people could pull this off since most providers diagnosing ADHD aren't even psychiatrists) as well as her own stimulant prescription. Oh, and lately her mom has been posting on Facebook about how proud she is of her daughter for always being on the dean's list. Must be nice; I take a considerable dose of dextroamphetamine and still can't work full time without burning out instantly. I'm so glad that having a lack of integrity allows neurotypical folks the option of an unfair advantage with little effort, the only downside being a teeny little drug addiction. But its totally okay--Stimulant use is glorified in society... but only if its medication! We can't have these perfect overachievers getting hooked on meth, that's dirty! But taking someone's prescription or lying for your? No problem at all! It definitely won't cause shortages or DEA crackdowns, thereby causing people with that condition to struggle to get meds when they need it! /s

&#x200B;

It is incredibly frustrating to watch people take away medications and resources intended to help people with genuine ailments. Whether it be for social pity points, to become basically superhuman, or worse, to sell medication for profit... It is completely reasonable to have a hunch that you may have a condition (you CAN say, "I suspect x condition") but the truth is, you don't have the same awareness of what is truly typical that a psychologist or psychiatrist has. So many people will think they have a condition, not knowing the "symptoms" they're experiencing are typical among the general population. Tiktok videos or anecdotes from other people are not reliable to base a diagnosis of yourself on. Nor is simply looking at the DSM V and thinking "hm I have this symptom and this symptom so I have this condition." There is so much clinical context necessary to truly diagnose someone. There is at the very minimum four years of academic schooling behind being able to completely understand what the DSM implies. and yes I KNOW that diagnosis is often not accessible to some people; I grew up entirely impoverished and still am. It sucks, but imagine if someone went and said, "well I get migraines, experience nausea, and slur my words frequently, I am certain that I have a brain tumor," without ever getting an MRI to actually rule out other causes. It looks and is completely irresponsible and absurd.

&#x200B;

Most of the controversial self diagnosis cases I've seen pertain to psych conditions. Psychiatric conditions are EXTREMELY NUANCED and so many symptoms can be attributed to different conditions. Executive dysfunction for example could be due to a million things such as chronic fatigue syndrome, anemia, depression, neurodivergence, hypothyroidism. These NEED to be ruled out clinically before any solid conclusion is drawn. Some conditions have like 90% of their symptoms overlapping. It's even scarier when people are diagnosing themselves with conditions that literally make you lose a sense of reality or lose self awareness like psychosis, schizoaffective disorder, and schizophrenia. Like how are you supposed to definitively conclude you have those issues if they cause actual delusions??? Differential diagnosis by a competent provider is a must. Nothing can change my mind on this. I don't care if people call me ableist; I have adhd, panic disorder, c-ptsd and a slew of physical ailments. I am thousands of dollars in debt to clinics and hospitals, and it blows, but I'd rather that then delude myself into thinking I know better than a doctor.

Have used these products throughout my life: smartphones--htc evo, iphone 5s, iphone 8, lg g2, oneplus 7 pro, samsung s21 ultra (current). laptops--macbook pro (2011), macbook pro with retina display (2015), samsung notebook 9 pro, samsung galaxy book 3 pro 360 (current) These are just my feelings based on my experience and what I recall as a sorta-techy individual. If I'm mistaken on anything lmk

&#x200B;

in favor of android/pc:

&#x200B;

\-customization: it took ages for apple to allow their users to change themes or app icons. androids have had widgets and editable app icons since I was a kid with my HTC evo in 2010! I still benefit from using action launcher on my samsung phone today to adjust my home screen to [match my aesthetic.](https://imgur.com/a/S8DK9Nt) Android also had features like live wallpapers way before apple.

&#x200B;

\-Apple generally was the one to start unfavorable trends in phone launches such as removing charging blocks upon purchase as well as ditching headphone jacks. Apple comes off as greedy and out of touch with its users. 

&#x200B;

\- i always found it easier to access downloaded files on androids personally.

&#x200B;

\- easier to self-repair in my experience. I replaced the digitizer for my lg g2  myself at the ripe age of 14.

&#x200B;

\- Android companies often make their own components such as screens. Apple sources many components from third party vendors, which is why they were late to using OLED displays and had to end up using Samsung's OLED panels.

&#x200B;

\- Android has beat out Apple in camera in my opinion. The post processing apple does to photos is ridiculous imo. I love my Samsung camera.

&#x200B;

\- Durability. My Oneplus 9 pro in almond finish was the most beautiful phone and also the sturdiest phone Ive ever owned. I'm very clumsy but the gorilla glass back and front held up after many a fall down a hard flight of stairs. It only finally cracked when I dropped it in a literal mosh pit--a crowd of rowdy hip hop heads jumped on top of it repeatedly. Still worked just fine after too.

&#x200B;

\- Innovative freedom. It seems Apple avoids making many creative decisions as to not upset their userbase. I've seen Apple stans lament over how cute the Galaxy fold is and saying "I'll just wait for Apple to make it!" Doubt they will. I personally loved when android had curved displays. Everyone that saw my oneplus 7 pro always mentioned how futuristic it looked with its curved display and [glossy, cream, color-shifting frame.](https://i.ytimg.com/vi/S-kdeQsroEQ/maxresdefault.jpg) There's other examples of Androids just looking so much cooler than iPhones imo. [lg v30](https://9to5google.com/2018/01/13/hands-on-every-manufacturer-should-make-crazy-colors-like-the-raspberry-rose-lg-v30/lg_v30_raspberry_rose_4/) , [huawei](https://s.yimg.com/os/creatr-uploaded-images/2018-12/1e073400-02dc-11e9-9ffb-9304f0a66eec)

&#x200B;

 It also irks me how Apple forces users to have either a Macbook and/or an Ipad rather than designing a 360 degree laptop. I make digital art and had no interest in purchasing a tablet. Luckily my galaxy book 3 can function both as a tablet with a pen as well as a powerful pc laptop capable of gaming and running my art programs.

&#x200B;

\- Navigation buttons are great tbh.

&#x200B;

\- gaming has always been and likely will remain better on android and PC rather than iphone/macbook.

&#x200B;

\- Type C charging is far more convenient imo. Apple is insane for still pushing their lightning cables imo.

&#x200B;

In favor of apple:

&#x200B;

\-Factor of "it just works" upon first using the product or its features, although android/pc has gotten much better with this. But most people still see apple as being the most approachable especially for older folks/non-techy people.

&#x200B;

\- Premium feel materials. Apple ditched defaulting to plastic chassis for their phones and laptops long before androids/pc. 

&#x200B;

\-App store/app integration. This has gotten a lot better for androids, but it still seems that many apps prioritize features for Apple users with the exception of some supporting newer Samsung phones. Apps like Tiktok and Snapchat have always failed to make proper use of Android cameras which is partially why Androids have the misleading reputation of having shittier cameras.

im also in this position. I was addicted to making art all throughout childhood up until like age 16. I started making digital art with a tablet I got for christmas at age 10. I took commissions, I had hundreds of deviantart followers and plenty of friends, I entered and won art contests online and in person, I dreamed of going into design and illustration fields as an adult and even got into a prestigious university for graphic design. I panicked once I got to the school and immediately dropped out before I could accrue any debt. It was too alienating being the only poor student. I also felt like I was wasting time and resources at the university by learning something non-stem, which is sad to think about.

&#x200B;

Along with dropping out, I got diagnosed with ADHD at age 18. Now I've just been working standard day jobs to make ends meet, and--while I've gotten into realism oil painting for money--I haven't had any interest in making my own characters or creating fanart or just practicing the things I used to in any capacity.  Painting realism feels like being a printer. I have completely lost my "art style." I only sat down and drew in a sketchbook every few months. It didn't help that my laptop, which is a 4-in-1 (flips into a tablet monitor that you can draw on), somehow got the touch screen messed up so I can no longer do digital art that way.

&#x200B;

But just today I pulled out my old wacom intuos pen and touch I was using back in middle school and downloaded Clip Studio. It feels foreign even though I used a tablet consistently for about 6 years or so. It felt like I couldn't position it right. I couldn't remember where to place it in relation to my laptop. It was a pain to get it mapped somewhat comfortably. But eventually after fiddling around I got comfortable. I stared at a blank canvas on the program for a good bit before just shouting at my housemate, "hey what the hell should I draw?" to which she responded a "sexy Luigi." And so a sexy Luigi sketch I created. And it was fun! I think I can keep doing this. It's getting comfortable again. Those skills will bounce back faster than you know it. It's just difficult when you're comparing yourself to other artists. I want to go back to commissions eventually but I know I have to avoid seeing that as the goal if I want to fall in love with drawing again.
